{"kind":"assert","text":"Decision on `6c0145a6`: yes. The fold of custody stays, as its own\nbackground reconciliation, outside the read-only acquisition.\n\n`Custody.folded` writes an outcome from a verified entry of the\njournal. Ruling `81711f39` already keeps outcome writes outside the\nfence of a member call. The fold changes no draft, so the rule that\na draft changes only by a call of the member holds.\n\n1. The acquisition for a publication reads and writes nothing. The\n   fold has its own owner and its own name in the source, and the\n   acquisition does not call it.\n2. A view does not depend on the fold. For a key with a verified\n   entry, the first two C3 rules give the status from the entry. So\n   the view is the same when the fold ran, when it failed and when\n   it has not run yet. One case pins that: the same publication\n   with the fold held back and with the fold done.\n3. A failed fold stops no publication and no member call. It runs\n   again at the next change of the replica or of the store, as\n   `#sync` does today.\n4. The fold keeps its present trigger set and its present writes.\n   C4 adds no new write to it.\n\nIf the source shows a view that does need the fold first, stop and\ntell me which status differs.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}